WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.445
NARRATOR: Begin by visualizing two distinct
execution environments operating

2
00:00:04.445 --> 00:00:06.887
simultaneously on the same hardware.

3
00:00:06.887 --> 00:00:12.335
NARRATOR: User mode handles everyday
application logic, while kernel mode manages

4
00:00:12.335 --> 00:00:15.306
hardware resources and enforces security
boundaries.

5
00:00:15.306 --> 00:00:20.777
NARRATOR: When a user program needs to access
protected services, it deliberately triggers a

6
00:00:20.777 --> 00:00:23.903
system call that transfers control into the
kernel.

7
00:00:23.903 --> 00:00:28.711
NARRATOR: This deliberate handoff prevents
unauthorized hardware manipulation.

8
00:00:28.711 --> 00:00:33.555
NARRATOR: Imagine a web browser requesting a
network socket; the browser code cannot

9
00:00:33.555 --> 00:00:35.790
directly talk to the network card.

10
00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:41.977
NARRATOR: Instead, it issues a system call,
causing the CPU to switch to kernel mode,

11
00:00:41.977 --> 00:00:46.926
where the OS validates the request and
executes the appropriate hardware driver.

12
00:00:46.926 --> 00:00:53.394
NARRATOR: This separation ensures that a buggy
application cannot crash the entire system or

13
00:00:53.394 --> 00:00:55.242
access another program's data.

14
00:00:55.242 --> 00:00:59.965
NARRATOR: The kernel acts as a strict
gatekeeper, only allowing operations that

15
00:00:59.965 --> 00:01:00.752
follow defined,

16
00:01:00.866 --> 00:01:06.232
NARRATOR: Next, observe how the kernel manages
multiple applications running on a single

17
00:01:06.232 --> 00:01:06.645
processor.

18
00:01:06.645 --> 00:01:11.680
NARRATOR: When one program finishes its
current task or waits for input, the scheduler

19
00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:13.838
must pause it and start another.

20
00:01:13.838 --> 00:01:16.759
NARRATOR: This transition is called a context
switch.

21
00:01:16.759 --> 00:01:22.669
NARRATOR: During this moment, the kernel saves
the complete register state of the paused

22
00:01:22.669 --> 00:01:25.624
process, including instruction pointers and
stack addresses.

23
00:01:25.624 --> 00:01:29.247
NARRATOR: It then restores the saved registers
of the next process.

24
00:01:29.247 --> 00:01:34.649
NARRATOR: Think of it like a theater stage
manager who saves every actor's costume and

25
00:01:34.649 --> 00:01:36.810
notes before calling the next performer.

26
00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:42.703
NARRATOR: If the kernel failed to save these
registers, the next program would resume with

27
00:01:42.703 --> 00:01:44.668
corrupted data or crash immediately.

28
00:01:44.668 --> 00:01:50.941
NARRATOR: The scheduler continuously evaluates
which process is ready to run, ensuring fair

29
00:01:50.941 --> 00:01:53.354
CPU distribution without manual intervention.

30
00:01:53.354 --> 00:01:59.039
NARRATOR: This automatic handoff keeps the
system responsive even when dozens of

31
00:01:59.039 --> 00:01:59.986
applications compete.

32
00:02:00.146 --> 00:02:05.079
NARRATOR: Consider how the CPU actually
recognizes when to pause user code and enter

33
00:02:05.079 --> 00:02:05.784
the kernel.

34
00:02:05.784 --> 00:02:09.522
NARRATOR: This happens through a hardware
mechanism called a trap.

35
00:02:09.522 --> 00:02:14.735
NARRATOR: Traps are not just for system calls;
they also handle device interrupts and memory

36
00:02:14.735 --> 00:02:15.083
faults.

37
00:02:15.083 --> 00:02:19.979
NARRATOR: When a trap occurs, the CPU
automatically switches to kernel mode and

38
00:02:19.979 --> 00:02:22.238
jumps to a specific handler routine.

39
00:02:22.238 --> 00:02:27.811
NARRATOR: Imagine a doorbell ringing in a
house: the resident (user mode) is

40
00:02:27.811 --> 00:02:32.098
interrupted, and the homeowner (kernel) comes
to check the source.

41
00:02:32.098 --> 00:02:37.504
NARRATOR: This unified trap mechanism
simplifies OS design because the same hardware

42
00:02:37.504 --> 00:02:42.009
signal can trigger different software
responses depending on the context.

43
00:02:42.009 --> 00:02:47.882
NARRATOR: Whether it is a keyboard press, a
disk error, or a network request, the trap

44
00:02:47.882 --> 00:02:51.919
ensures the kernel intercepts the event before
it disrupts normal execution.

45
00:02:52.034 --> 00:02:58.372
NARRATOR: Now, examine how the OS maps the
vast virtual address space of a program to

46
00:02:58.372 --> 00:02:59.560
limited physical memory.

47
00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:04.573
NARRATOR: Each process maintains its own page
table, which acts like a detailed directory.

48
00:03:04.573 --> 00:03:09.896
NARRATOR: When the program accesses a specific
virtual address, the hardware consults this

49
00:03:09.896 --> 00:03:12.762
table to find the corresponding physical
location.

50
00:03:12.762 --> 00:03:17.975
NARRATOR: If the page is not currently loaded
in RAM, the kernel triggers a page fault and

51
00:03:17.975 --> 00:03:19.815
loads the required data from disk.

52
00:03:19.815 --> 00:03:25.333
NARRATOR: This translation happens
transparently, allowing programs to use large,

53
00:03:25.333 --> 00:03:30.300
contiguous address spaces without worrying
about physical memory constraints.

54
00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:35.657
NARRATOR: Imagine a library catalog that
translates shelf locations into actual room

55
00:03:35.657 --> 00:03:36.103
numbers.

56
00:03:36.103 --> 00:03:41.866
NARRATOR: Without this system, programs would
need to manually calculate every memory

57
00:03:41.866 --> 00:03:44.267
address, making development nearly impossible.

58
00:03:44.267 --> 00:03:49.936
NARRATOR: The page table ensures that virtual
addresses remain stable while physical memory

59
00:03:49.936 --> 00:03:51.244
is dynamically managed.

60
00:03:51.386 --> 00:03:57.584
NARRATOR: Virtual memory extends this
translation system to guarantee strict process

61
00:03:57.584 --> 00:03:58.147
isolation.

62
00:03:58.147 --> 00:04:03.818
NARRATOR: Each process receives its own
private address space, meaning one program

63
00:04:03.818 --> 00:04:07.126
cannot accidentally or maliciously read
another's data.

64
00:04:07.126 --> 00:04:12.354
NARRATOR: The kernel controls which physical
pages are mapped to which virtual addresses,

65
00:04:12.354 --> 00:04:14.767
allowing it to enforce security boundaries.

66
00:04:14.767 --> 00:04:20.570
NARRATOR: When a process terminates, the
kernel simply removes its page table entries,

67
00:04:20.570 --> 00:04:22.356
instantly freeing the memory.

68
00:04:22.356 --> 00:04:27.394
NARRATOR: This design enables multiple
applications to run simultaneously without

69
00:04:27.394 --> 00:04:30.417
interfering with each other's data structures.

70
00:04:30.417 --> 00:04:35.268
NARRATOR: Imagine separate rooms in a building
where each resident has their own private

71
00:04:35.268 --> 00:04:38.733
space, but the building manager controls which
rooms are open.

72
00:04:38.733 --> 00:04:44.040
NARRATOR: This isolation prevents data
corruption and ensures system stability, even

73
00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:46.935
when applications crash or behave
unpredictably.

74
00:04:47.066 --> 00:04:50.434
NARRATOR: Let's trace how a new process begins
execution.

75
00:04:50.434 --> 00:04:55.478
NARRATOR: When a program calls fork, the
kernel creates a new process image that is a

76
00:04:55.478 --> 00:04:57.054
logical copy of the parent.

77
00:04:57.054 --> 00:05:02.743
NARRATOR: The child receives a unique process
identifier and starts executing independently.

78
00:05:02.743 --> 00:05:07.653
NARRATOR: This duplication happens at the
process level, not the memory level.

79
00:05:07.653 --> 00:05:13.015
NARRATOR: Imagine a photocopy machine that
duplicates the entire document but allows the

80
00:05:13.015 --> 00:05:15.077
copy to be edited separately.

81
00:05:15.077 --> 00:05:21.234
NARRATOR: The parent and child now follow
different execution paths, each managing its

82
00:05:21.234 --> 00:05:22.181
own resources.

83
00:05:22.181 --> 00:05:27.092
NARRATOR: This mechanism enables parallel
execution and modular program design.

84
00:05:27.092 --> 00:05:32.175
NARRATOR: The kernel ensures that the child
process has all the necessary initialization

85
00:05:32.175 --> 00:05:36.867
data to start correctly, while maintaining
strict separation from the parent's subsequent

86
00:05:36.867 --> 00:05:37.258
operations.

87
00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:42.850
NARRATOR: After the fork operation completes,
the parent and child processes receive

88
00:05:42.850 --> 00:05:44.220
different return values.

89
00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:49.847
NARRATOR: This distinction allows each process
to immediately determine its role and choose

90
00:05:49.847 --> 00:05:51.146
its execution path.

91
00:05:51.146 --> 00:05:55.808
NARRATOR: The parent typically continues with
its original task, while the child branches

92
00:05:55.808 --> 00:05:57.243
into a new routine.

93
00:05:57.243 --> 00:06:02.408
NARRATOR: Imagine a traffic light system where
the main controller sends a signal to a side

94
00:06:02.408 --> 00:06:05.636
car, allowing the side car to take a different
route.

95
00:06:05.636 --> 00:06:10.910
NARRATOR: This return value mechanism enables
flexible program architecture, allowing

96
00:06:10.910 --> 00:06:16.184
developers to implement complex workflows
without hardcoding every possible scenario.

97
00:06:16.184 --> 00:06:21.742
NARRATOR: The kernel ensures that the return
values are correctly passed through the system

98
00:06:21.742 --> 00:06:25.712
call interface, maintaining the expected
behavior across different operating systems.

99
00:06:25.850 --> 00:06:30.850
NARRATOR: When a process is created, it
inherits certain resources from its parent,

100
00:06:30.850 --> 00:06:33.542
such as file descriptors and memory mappings.

101
00:06:33.542 --> 00:06:38.362
NARRATOR: These inherited resources follow
strict sharing rules that determine whether

102
00:06:38.362 --> 00:06:40.991
they are duplicated, shared, or restricted.

103
00:06:40.991 --> 00:06:46.529
NARRATOR: Imagine a family recipe book where
each family member gets a copy, but some pages

104
00:06:46.529 --> 00:06:48.606
are marked as shared for collaboration.

105
00:06:48.606 --> 00:06:53.628
NARRATOR: The kernel enforces these rules to
prevent resource leaks and ensure proper

106
00:06:53.628 --> 00:06:54.014
cleanup.

107
00:06:54.014 --> 00:06:58.290
NARRATOR: If a parent closes a file
descriptor, the child may still access it

108
00:06:58.290 --> 00:06:59.817
depending on the sharing mode.

109
00:06:59.817 --> 00:07:05.096
NARRATOR: This inheritance model allows
applications to pass resources between

110
00:07:05.096 --> 00:07:09.320
processes efficiently while maintaining
control over access permissions.

111
00:07:09.320 --> 00:07:13.522
NARRATOR: The kernel tracks these references
to ensure they are released when no longer

112
00:07:13.522 --> 00:07:13.822
needed.

113
00:07:13.970 --> 00:07:19.366
NARRATOR: To improve efficiency, many systems
implement copy-on-write virtual memory.

114
00:07:19.366 --> 00:07:24.813
NARRATOR: Instead of duplicating all memory
pages when a process forks, the kernel shares

115
00:07:24.813 --> 00:07:27.925
the same physical pages between parent and
child.

116
00:07:27.925 --> 00:07:31.700
NARRATOR: Pages are only copied when one
process attempts to write to them.

117
00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:38.226
NARRATOR: Imagine two readers sharing a single
textbook; they can read simultaneously without

118
00:07:38.226 --> 00:07:38.728
duplication.

119
00:07:38.728 --> 00:07:44.697
NARRATOR: If one reader tries to edit a page,
the system creates a private copy for that

120
00:07:44.697 --> 00:07:45.399
specific page.

121
00:07:45.399 --> 00:07:50.743
NARRATOR: This optimization reduces memory
usage and speeds up process creation.

122
00:07:50.743 --> 00:07:55.759
NARRATOR: However, the kernel must carefully
track write attempts to ensure the original

123
00:07:55.759 --> 00:07:58.460
data remains intact for the other process.

124
00:07:58.460 --> 00:08:04.049
NARRATOR: This approach preserves the expected
process behavior while maximizing resource

125
00:08:04.049 --> 00:08:04.557
efficiency.

126
00:08:04.682 --> 00:08:09.639
NARRATOR: Finally, consider the complete
lifecycle of a process from creation to

127
00:08:09.639 --> 00:08:10.052
termination.

128
00:08:10.052 --> 00:08:15.109
NARRATOR: The kernel manages execution
replacement, waiting states, and termination

129
00:08:15.109 --> 00:08:18.649
events, ensuring that resources are properly
reclaimed.

130
00:08:18.649 --> 00:08:24.136
NARRATOR: If a process crashes or exits
unexpectedly, the kernel must handle the

131
00:08:24.136 --> 00:08:27.935
failure gracefully, cleaning up memory and
releasing file descriptors.

132
00:08:27.935 --> 00:08:33.299
NARRATOR: Imagine a theater production where
actors leave early; the stage manager must

133
00:08:33.299 --> 00:08:36.188
reset the set and notify other performers.

134
00:08:36.188 --> 00:08:42.050
NARRATOR: The kernel tracks every resource
ownership and failure case to maintain system

135
00:08:42.050 --> 00:08:42.501
stability.

136
00:08:42.501 --> 00:08:47.263
NARRATOR: This disciplined lifecycle
management prevents memory leaks and ensures

137
00:08:47.263 --> 00:08:52.501
that the operating system remains responsive
even when applications encounter errors.
